{"kind":"request","text":"Make the UI's work counts and `gs status` count the same populations, or say plainly that they do not. Observed from GitHub issue #1 (observation aa31d08e), filed by hughpyle and admitted under clause 0bb7957c. This is the first issue to come through the connector, and filing this request is the member decision the charter reserves — the observation itself obligates nobody.\n\nNot a duplicate, though it is adjacent to one. I checked the live open requests and the whole statement log: nothing tracks this. It is related to be558440, which stops the merge step manufacturing staleness, because most of what the UI is currently counting as needing attention is that manufactured staleness — but fixing be558440 would change the numbers without addressing why the two surfaces disagree.\n\nThe disagreement is real and it is a methodology difference, not a bug in either count.\n\n`gs status` reports commitments by status and artifacts on a separate line: 485 commitments across eight statuses, then 218 current and 157 stale artifacts. It never adds the two together.\n\nThe UI's three numbers are not that partition. Active is commitments whose status is open, promised or reported (ui/src/lib/work.ts, ACTIVE_WORK_STATUSES). Closed is satisfied, withdrawn, cancelled or reneged. Attention, though, is commitments the item-state calls attention **plus** `otherWorkAttention`, which counts retired-or-stale *artifacts* and dangling promises. So Attention mixes two populations the CLI keeps apart, which is why 355 does not correspond to any line in the status output and why the three UI numbers sum to more than the commitment count.\n\nNeither surface is wrong on its own terms. What is wrong is that a reader comparing them cannot tell they are answering different questions, and the natural conclusion — that one of them is miscounting — is false.\n\nI have not decided which way it should resolve, and I do not think I should. Making the UI partition commitments exactly as the CLI does would make the numbers comparable and lose the artifact signal the drawer exists to surface. Keeping the mixed count and labelling it honestly keeps the signal and admits the numbers are not comparable. That is a product judgement about what the drawer is for.","body":{"conditions":"A reader comparing the UI work counts with `gs status` can tell whether the two are answering the same question: either the UI partitions commitments exactly as the CLI does and surfaces artifact attention as a separately labelled figure, or the mixed count is retained and labelled so that it plainly does not correspond to a CLI line; whichever is chosen, the decision and its reason are recorded rather than left implicit in the code; the counting rule is stated in the shipped documentation next to whichever surface a reader meets first; a test pins the chosen relationship between the two so the surfaces cannot drift apart again silently; `make test`, `go vet` and the UI gates pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}